@startuml
title R3 debug control - the four-state machine, STOP-BEFORE landing, the opcode flows, and the bring-up trap
top to bottom direction
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam legendBackgroundColor #f8f8f8
skinparam legendBorderColor #666666
hide stereotype

skinparam component {
  BackgroundColor<<free>> #d5e8d4
  BorderColor<<free>> #82b366
  FontColor<<free>> #1d4620
  BackgroundColor<<held>> #ffe6cc
  BorderColor<<held>> #d79b00
  FontColor<<held>> #7f4b00
  BackgroundColor<<hit>> #f8cecc
  BorderColor<<hit>> #b85450
  FontColor<<hit>> #7b1c18
  BackgroundColor<<enc>> #dbe5f1
  BorderColor<<enc>> #6c8ebf
  FontColor<<enc>> #1f3864
  BackgroundColor<<trap>> #e1d5e7
  BorderColor<<trap>> #9673a6
  FontColor<<trap>> #4a235a
}

package "THE STATE ENCODING - one wire, and R2's old 0/1 keep their meaning" {
  component "dbg_state = dbg_hold_r ? (bp_hit ? 3 : 2)\n                         : (run     ? 1 : 0)\nbp_flags  = {bp_hit, bp_en}\n  bit0 = breakpoint ARMED\n  bit1 = HIT latched" as enc <<enc>>

  component "0  STOPPED\nthe normal boot stop:\nrun=0, no hold,\nPC HELD AT 0" as s0 <<free>>

  component "1  RUNNING\nthe strap is high and\nno debug hold is\nasserted" as s1 <<free>>

  component "2  DEBUG_HOLD\nheld by the debug controls\nwith the PC PRESERVED\n(single-stepping).\nThe core is NOT executing." as s2 <<held>>

  component "3  BP_HIT\nheld by the breakpoint:\nPC preserved,\nthe hit LATCHED" as s3 <<hit>>

  component "bp_addr is the armed address, or 0 when disarmed.\nA BREAKPOINT AT ADDRESS 0 IS LEGAL and is told\napart from 'disarmed' by bp_flags bit0, not by\nthe address - which is the whole reason the flag\nexists." as addr <<enc>>

  enc --> s0
  enc --> s1
  enc --> s2
  enc --> s3
  s3 --> addr
}

package "THE MACHINE - every edge is one of the four opcodes, or the strap, or reset" {
  component "0 STOPPED" as t0 <<free>>
  component "1 RUNNING" as t1 <<free>>
  component "2 DEBUG_HOLD" as t2 <<held>>
  component "3 BP_HIT" as t3 <<hit>>

  component "BP_CLR  -  THE ONLY RELEASE\nreleases the hold AND disarms.\nwith run=1 the core RESUMES (state 1);\nwith run=0 it returns to the normal boot\nstop (state 0, PC re-zeroed at that same\nedge). IDEMPOTENT - safe when disarmed." as tclr <<enc>>

  component "DEBUG_STEP  -  executes EXACTLY ONE\ninstruction, then returns to held.\nFrom 0 or 2. NOT_READY, with NO fault\nand NO side effect, while the core is\nFREE-RUNNING (run=1 and not held)." as tstep <<enc>>

  t0 --> t1 : raise the run strap
  t1 --> t0 : drop the run strap
  t0 --> t2 : DEBUG_STEP
  t2 --> t2 : DEBUG_STEP (the hit clears)
  t2 --> t3 : DEBUG_STEP that LANDS on the bp
  t1 --> t3 : a live core's landing PC\nmatches an armed bp_addr
  t3 --> t2 : DEBUG_STEP that does NOT land
  t1 --> t1 : BP_CLR
  t2 --> t1 : BP_CLR, run=1 - RESUMES
  t3 --> t1 : BP_CLR, run=1 - RESUMES
  t2 --> t0 : BP_CLR, run=0
  t3 --> t0 : BP_CLR, run=0
  tclr -[hidden]down-> tstep
}

package "STOP-BEFORE - the hit compares the LANDING address, not the current one" {
  component "THE COMPARISON\nif (bp_en && !dbg_hold_r && (run || dbg_step)\n    && (dbg_next_pc == bp_addr))\n  begin bp_hit <= 1; dbg_hold_r <= 1; end\n\ndbg_next_pc is the combinational next PC - the\naddress the core is ABOUT to execute." as cmp <<enc>>

  component "SO THE CORE IS HELD AT THAT EDGE WITH\nPC == bp_addr, AND THE INSTRUCTION AT bp_addr\nHAS NOT RUN. No io_we, no dmem_we, no a/x/y\neffect of it occurred. That is the stop-before a\ndebugger expects: inspect it, then step THAT\nvery instruction." as sb <<hit>>

  component "AND STOP-BEFORE IS NOT A NO-OP STEP.\nStepping 1 to 2 with the breakpoint armed at 2\nDOES execute the LDI at 1 - a step retires the\ninstruction it stepped FROM, as a step always\ndoes - and leaves the instruction AT 2 untouched.\nIt protects the one the debugger is about to\ninspect, and that is the entire distinction." as sb2 <<held>>

  component "THE PC IS PRESERVED, NOT RE-ZEROED\nUnder a hold the PC keeps its value across\narbitrarily many cycles. Only the boot stop\n(run=0, no hold) re-zeroes it, exactly as R2's\nread arbitration requires." as sb3 <<held>>

  component "THE FETCH FOLLOWS THE SAME THREE MODES\nimem_addr = cpu_exec ? next_pc\n            : dbg_hold ? pc\n            : 0\nso each mode keeps the pipeline invariant R2's\nread arbitration depends on, and insn follows the\nmode rather than being imem[pc] unconditionally." as sb4 <<enc>>

  cmp --> sb
  sb --> sb2
  sb2 --> sb3
  sb3 --> sb4
}

package "THE FOUR OPCODE FLOWS - TARGET_HOST only; the loopback target answers UNSUPPORTED" {
  component "0x21 DEBUG_STEP   len 0\n  -> (OK, state, pc_next, bp_addr, bp_flags)\n  5 words.\n  state and bp_flags are the state AFTER the step:\n  a step that lands on the armed address reports\n  state 3 and flags 0x03.\n  bp_flags here is computed POST-step, NOT the\n  pre-edge latch, which would report the hit the\n  step is clearing.\n  On refusal: NOT_READY, state/pc from the live\n  registers, and the PRE-edge bp_flags." as f_step <<held>>

  component "0x22 DEBUG_BP_SET  len 1\n  -> (OK, state, pc, bp_addr, bp_flags)\n  5 words. ONE payload word: the address.\n  address must be < IMEM_WORDS, else RANGE, and\n  then the breakpoint is NOT armed and NOT\n  changed - a rejected op has no side effect.\n  On RANGE the bp_addr and bp_flags words report\n  the UNCHANGED previous values, not the request.\n  On OK: bp_addr is the armed address, flags 0x01.\n  Arming CLEARS A STALE HIT. May arm while running." as f_set <<enc>>

  component "0x23 DEBUG_BP_CLR  len 0\n  -> (OK, state, pc, bp_addr_before, bp_flags)\n  5 words. Disarms, clears the hit, and RELEASES\n  the hold. state is the RELEASED state: 1 with the\n  strap high, 0 with it low. pc is the PC AT THE\n  REQUEST - with run=0 the core re-zeroes at that\n  same edge, so the FOLLOWING read reports 0.\n  This is also how a host resumes a core that a\n  breakpoint stopped." as f_clr <<free>>

  component "0x24 DEBUG_STATUS  len 0\n  -> (OK, state, pc, bp_addr, bp_flags,\n                run, a, x, y, insn)\n  10 words: the common 5-word prefix plus the\n  architectural state, same field meanings and\n  widths as READ_CPU. run is the STRAP level.\n  ANSWERED WHILE RUNNING AND WHILE HELD." as f_stat <<enc>>

  f_step --> f_set : f_set --> f_clr : f_clr --> f_stat
}

package "THE BRING-UP TRAP - once held, the run strap is ignored BOTH WAYS" {
  component "THE GATE\ncpu_exec = (dbg_step === 1'b1)\n        || (run && !(dbg_hold === 1'b1))\n\nrun is MASKED BY THE HOLD - in BOTH directions." as g1 <<trap>>

  component "DROPPING run DOES NOT RESUME\nand RAISING run DOES NOT EITHER.\nA host that single-steps, or that stops on a\nbreakpoint, and then 're-asserts run=1 to carry\non' finds the core STILL HELD - with no fault\nand nothing in the response words to explain it." as g2 <<trap>>

  component "dbg_hold_r IS CLEARED AT EXACTLY TWO PLACES\n  1. reset\n  2. the DEBUG_BP_CLR branch\nand SET by a step or by a hit. Nowhere else. So\nthe ONLY resume is DEBUG_BP_CLR - which also\nDISARMS - or a reset.\nThis is a property of the debug design, not a bug." as g3 <<trap>>

  component "THE RESUME RECIPE, if the breakpoint\nis still wanted\n  1. DEBUG_STEP   step off the breakpoint; the\n                  hit clears, the core stays held\n  2. DEBUG_BP_CLR release; with run=1 it resumes\n  3. DEBUG_BP_SET re-arm WHILE IT RUNS\nThe core cannot instantly re-hit: it left bp_addr\nwhen it stepped, and the strap resumes it from the\nstep's landing address. BP_SET while running\nclears any stale hit, so the recipe is IDEMPOTENT.\nA host that does not care about the breakpoint\njust issues DEBUG_BP_CLR once." as g4 <<enc>>

  g1 --> g2
  g2 --> g3
  g3 --> g4
}

package "THE SUBTLETY: BP_SET clears bp_en and bp_hit but NOT dbg_hold_r" {
  component "DEBUG_BP_CLR clears ALL THREE\nbp_en, bp_hit AND dbg_hold_r" as c1 <<free>>

  component "DEBUG_BP_SET clears only TWO\nbp_en and bp_hit - NOT dbg_hold_r.\nSo arming a core that is ALREADY HELD does\nnot release it, and nothing reports an error." as c2 <<hit>>

  component "WHY THAT MATTERS: the free-running hit\nlatch is gated on !dbg_hold_r, so an armed\nbreakpoint cannot produce a hit on a core that\nis held and therefore not advancing. The core\nsits at state 2 with bp_hit clear, forever." as c3 <<hit>>

  component "BUT DO NOT GENERALISE IT. The STEP path\nis NOT gated on !dbg_hold_r - DEBUG_STEP sets\nbp_hit directly from (bp_en && dbg_next_pc ==\nbp_addr). So ARMING A HELD CORE IS LEGAL, and\nstepping onto the armed address DOES latch\nstate 2 to 3. That is exactly the stop-before\nflow the R2 held-core vectors use." as c4 <<held>>

  component "WHICH IS WHY THE VECTORS OPEN WITH\nDEBUG_BP_CLR. The first v10 prep re-armed a\ncore the v09 step had just left held; the hit\nnever latched and the failure surfaced as a WORD\nMISMATCH INSIDE A GOLDEN RESPONSE - word 5 =\n0002, want 0003 - pointing at the STATUS builder\nwhen the cause was two opcodes earlier. Both preps\nnow open with BP_CLR and ASSERT the release." as c5 <<enc>>

  c1 --> c2
  c2 --> c3
  c3 --> c4
  c4 --> c5
}

' LAYOUT ONLY, no architecture. The six sections read in order - the encoding,
' the machine, the landing rule, the opcodes, the trap, the subtlety - but as
' siblings they lay out as columns and the diagram goes wide. This hidden
' chain stacks them so each section adds height rather than width, which is
' the squarer-aspect invariant in diagrams/README.md. Safe to delete.
addr -[hidden]down-> t0
tclr -[hidden]down-> cmp
sb4 -[hidden]down-> f_step
f_stat -[hidden]down-> g1
g4 -[hidden]down-> c1

note bottom of enc
  THE ENCODING IS 2 BITS IN THE LOW HALF of the
  word and the UPPER 14 ARE ZERO, so a host can
  read it as a small integer without masking and
  a swapped 2/3 encoding is still a valid-looking
  word. That is exactly why it needed a vector:
  a mutant swapping 2 and 3 still passes the 18
  shipped R2 steps and fails only the four
  held-core ones - 18/22, the four held steps and
  nothing else.
end note

note bottom of enc
  bp_flags bit0 is what makes 'disarmed' and
  'armed at address 0' different states, and it is
  also the only thing a host can trust after an
  out-of-range BP_SET, which leaves both the
  register and the reported flag untouched.
end note

note bottom of cmp
  THE FREE-RUNNING LATCH AND THE STEP PATH ARE
  TWO DIFFERENT PLACES, and collapsing them into
  one sentence is how a host ends up encoding a
  half-truth as a rule. The free-running latch
  (pe_ctrl.v:692) is gated on !dbg_hold_r; the
  step path (:1067) is not. A guard written from
  the over-broad reading refuses to arm a held
  core - which is LEGAL - and its own conformance
  run is what caught it.
end note

note bottom of f_step
  All four debug ops are READY-IMMEDIATE: they
  are answered from registers in the request's own
  CRC cycle, exactly like READ_CPU, so they emit
  ZERO 0xFFFF filler words. The wait-word rule
  stays what it is for the bounded reads 0x13 and
  0x14 ONLY. A host must not assume every op can
  return late.

  NO NEW FAULT CLASS. A malformed frame (bad CRC,
  bad length, bad header) answers BAD_FRAME and
  has NO side effect - no step, no arming. A
  sequencing refusal answers NOT_READY with no
  fault, exactly like LOAD while running.
end note

note bottom of g1
  WRITE THE GATE IN THE === FORM. The plain
  expression (dbg_step || (run && !dbg_hold))
  is the PRE-REPAIR form and it is NOT
  equivalent in simulation: with a debug input
  floating at x or z, !dbg_hold is x, so the
  gate goes x and the core freezes. The === 1'b1
  comparisons make an UNDRIVEN DEBUG INPUT READ
  AS INACTIVE - the step term false, the hold
  term inactive - so an unconnected port cannot
  stop the core. Ground truth: pe_cpu.v:233.

  The convention is enforced WHERE THE INPUT IS
  CONSUMED, not at every call site, because that
  is the only place a new call site cannot forget
  it. In synthesis the two forms are identical (x
  and z do not exist in hardware), so nothing
  about the netlist changes.

  This is not a theoretical concern. Leaving the
  debug inputs undriven in testbenches that
  instantiate pe_cpu/pe_soc directly took SIX ACTS
  to their reset after the 5b4731f merge, and the
  FULL SUITE caught it immediately - no unit TB
  finds a port addition on its own. The contract recorded 13 TBs tying
  them low; that count is DATED - on today's tree 26 testbenches
  reference pe_cpu/pe_soc and 21 of those tie the debug inputs low,
  because the population has grown. Quote it as the contract's figure,
  not as the current one.
end note

note bottom of g2
  This is invisible from the response words, which
  is why it is drawn with its own emphasis. It is
  recorded in the contract precisely because it
  costs a bring-up board a long time when nobody
  has written it down - and because a host whose
  MODEL declares debug_hold: true can never show
  it: the model reaches the state by fiat, the
  chip reaches it by traffic.
end note

note bottom of c5
  The second finding: BP_SET clears the latched
  bp_hit, so re-running the pre-state prep before a
  vector's SECOND step would turn state 3 back
  into state 2. The prep is therefore emitted once
  per VECTOR, not once per step - a golden read
  does not disturb the debug state, so one call per
  vector is enough. That is why the include is
  generated from the step table rather than
  hand-written.
end note

legend left
  |= Tone |= Meaning |
  | <#d5e8d4> | A state or field the strap alone controls |
  | <#ffe6cc> | A debug hold - PC preserved, core not executing |
  | <#f8cecc> | The breakpoint, and the traps around it |
  | <#dbe5f1> | Encoding, opcode shape, or a comparison |
  | <#e1d5e7> | The bring-up trap - the masked run strap |
endlegend

@enduml
